.TH BABYSITD 8 "2026-11-15" "babysit" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
babysitd \- housekeeping service for babysit logs
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B babysitd
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B babysitd
looks after the log directory used by
.BR babysit (1).
It is meant to run as a systemd user service.
It does not poll: it sleeps for
.B check_interval
(6 hours by default), does the things below when it wakes, and goes
back to sleep.
It can be woken early; see
.BR "EARLY REQUESTS" .
.PP
.B babysit
files the log of each instance under
.IR LOGDIR / YYYYmmdd / HH / MM / salt /log ,
by the minute it started in.
.TP
.B Archive past days
Every day directory before today's is packed, as far as it is finished,
into
.PP
.RS
.RS
.IR LOGDIR /archive/babysit_logs_ YYYYmmdd .tar.gz
.RE
.RE
.IP
and then removed.
The check is made on start and at every wake-up, so it does not matter
when the service runs.
An existing archive is never overwritten; a second one for the same day
gets a
.B .2
before the extension.
Archives are made with
.BR tar (1);
if that fails the directory is left in place and a warning is logged.
.IP
An instance whose log is still being written is left out of the archive and
stays exactly where it is, so its identifier goes on working; everything
else from that day is archived without waiting for it.
At each later pass the service looks again, and the first one that finds
the instance finished archives it too, in an archive of its own for that
day
.RB ( .2 ,
.B .3
and so on).
A command that runs for a week therefore keeps only its own log out of the
archive for that week.
"Finished" means that the babysitter has stopped writing the log, not that
an exit status was recorded: a log can still be growing after its command
has exited, and an instance whose babysitter was killed never gets a status
at all.
.TP
.B Pack records dated later than now
If the clock has been set back, the log directory holds records dated
later than now, and
.BR babysit (1)
refuses to start anything while they are in the way (see
.B "WHEN THE CLOCK GOES BACKWARDS"
there).
The service clears them: later days go to the archive like past ones, and
within today each later hour, and each later minute of the current hour,
is packed in place as
.IB HH .tar.gz
or
.IB MM .tar.gz .
An instance that is still being written is left in place, and
.B babysit
does not hold it against a new one; it is packed, like any other, by the
first pass that finds it finished.
.TP
.B Pack early when the disk fills up
Whenever it wakes, the service checks how full the filesystem holding
.I LOGDIR
is.
At
.B compact_at_disk_percent
or more it does not wait for today to be over either: every hour of today
that is over is packed in place, as far as it is finished, as
.IR YYYYmmdd / HH .tar.gz .
An instance still being written stays in its hour directory and is packed
later, as with days.
Packing compresses logs; it does not delete any.
On a filesystem that simply stays above the limit, this means that at every
wake-up the logs of the hours finished since the last one move into
tarballs; see
.BR "READING PACKED LOGS" .
.TP
.B Delete under disk pressure
This is off unless
.B delete_at_disk_percent
is set.
When the filesystem is at least that full, the service works out how much
must go to bring usage back under the limit, and deletes packed logs one at
a time, oldest first, until that much has gone or none are left.
It goes by the sizes of the files it deletes and does not measure the
filesystem again in between, because some filesystems (btrfs, ZFS) only
give space back a while after a file is deleted.
On those the reported usage can stay over the limit for a short time after
a pass that deleted enough.
Where snapshots keep deleted files alive, deleting frees nothing, and each
pass will delete another batch for no gain; do not set this option there.
The candidates are the archives and the packed hours; a live log directory
is never deleted, so a command that is still running, and logs not yet
packed, are safe.
Each deletion is logged as a warning.
If usage is still over the limit once every packed log is gone, the rest of
the space belongs to something else, and that is logged too.
.TP
.B Expire old logs
Archives and packed hours whose file is older than
.B max_age_days
are deleted, as are finished logs that old which are still in a day
directory because something else there is being written.
.PP
In short: packing collects the logs of finished commands, whichever they
are, and nothing else.
The dates are only how the files are organised.
A long-running command's log is not collected until the command has
finished, however long ago it started, so it is not in the archive that was
made for its day or hour if it was still running when that archive was
made.
The date in a tarball's name says when the runs inside it started; it does
not promise that every run started then is inside.
The ones that were still going are in a later tarball of the same date,
with a number before the extension, or still in place.
.PP
A log that still has a writer is never archived or deleted.
Any doubt counts as a writer: a log the service cannot open, and a log on
a filesystem that cannot report locks (some network and FUSE filesystems),
is left where it is, and where the service cannot look inside a directory
at all it leaves the whole of it.
Nothing created in the last two seconds is packed either, so that an
instance which has made its directory but not yet opened its log does not
find it gone.
.PP
"Today" and "this hour" are reckoned in local time, or in UTC if
.BR babysit.conf (5)
says
.BR "timezone = utc" ,
the same setting
.BR babysit (1)
names its directories by.
.PP
The service only ever packs or deletes what
.BR babysit (1)
and it created: directories named like a day
.RI ( YYYYmmdd ),
the two-digit hour and minute directories and
.IB NN .tar.gz
files inside them, and
.BI babysit_logs_ * .tar.gz
files in
.IR LOGDIR /archive .
Anything else found in
.I LOGDIR
is left alone.
It is still best to give
.B babysit
a directory of its own.
.PP
Only one
.B babysitd
works on a log directory at a time.
Each pass is made holding a lock on
.IR LOGDIR /.lock ,
so a second instance, such as a
.B \-\-once
run by hand or from cron while the service is running, waits for the pass in
progress to finish and then makes its own.
.PP
Messages go to stderr, which under systemd is the journal:
.PP
.RS
journalctl \-\-user \-u babysitd
.RE
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI "\-l, \-\-log\-dir " DIR
Log directory to look after.
Default:
.IR $HOME/.local/var/log/babysit .
.TP
.BI "\-c, \-\-config " FILE
Configuration file; see
.BR babysit.conf (5).
Default:
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/babysit/babysit.conf ,
or
.IR $HOME/.config/babysit/babysit.conf .
A missing default file is not an error.
.TP
.BI "\-a, \-\-max\-age\-days " N
Overrides
.B max_age_days
from the configuration file.
0 keeps logs forever.
Default: 30.
.TP
.BI "\-i, \-\-check\-interval " T
Overrides
.B check_interval
from the configuration file.
Default: 6h.
.TP
.BI "\-u, \-\-compact\-at\-disk\-percent " N
Overrides
.B compact_at_disk_percent
from the configuration file.
0 disables.
Default: 90.
.TP
.BI "\-D, \-\-delete\-at\-disk\-percent " N
Overrides
.B delete_at_disk_percent
from the configuration file.
0 disables.
Default: 0.
.TP
.B \-1, \-\-once
Do the housekeeping once, then exit.
Useful from cron, or by hand when
.BR babysit (1)
has exited with status 75.
.TP
.B \-V, \-\-version
Show the version.
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Show a summary of options.
.PP
Options that take a value accept it as the next argument, or after
.B =
in the long form.
Short options cannot be combined or have the value attached:
.B "\-a 14"
and
.BR \-\-max\-age\-days=14 ,
not
.BR \-a14 .
.SH READING PACKED LOGS
Packed logs keep their paths inside the tarball, relative to the directory
the tarball is in.
To list what an archive or a packed hour holds, and to print one log
without unpacking the rest:
.PP
.RS
.nf
tar \-tzf  $LOGDIR/archive/babysit_logs_20261116.tar.gz
tar \-xzOf $LOGDIR/archive/babysit_logs_20261116.tar.gz 20261116/06/15/k3f9/log
tar \-xzOf $LOGDIR/20261116/06.tar.gz 06/15/k3f9/log
.fi
.RE
.PP
An archived day may itself contain packed hours.
.PP
The
.BR babysit (1)
commands that take an identifier do not look inside tarballs.
.SH EARLY REQUESTS
The service listens on the FIFO
.IR LOGDIR /.request .
A byte written there wakes it at once for its whole housekeeping pass,
exactly as at a scheduled wake-up.
.BR babysit (1)
writes one when it refuses to start because of records dated later than
now, so that a retry finds them packed.
.PP
The write never blocks and its failure is ignored, so
.B babysit
behaves the same whether or not the service is running.
Anything may wake the service the same way, which is also how to test it:
lay out a log directory by hand, then
.PP
.RS
echo 1 > $LOGDIR/.request
.RE
.PP
What is written does not matter.
Only do this while the service is running: a shell opening a FIFO that has
no reader waits until one appears.
.SH FAILURES
Packing needs room for the tarball before the directory it replaces can be
removed, so it can fail on a disk that is already full.
The service is written so that this, or being killed part-way, loses
nothing:
.IP \(bu 2
A tarball is written under a
.B .partial
name and renamed into place only when complete; the directory is removed
only after that.
.IP \(bu 2
If packing fails, the partial file is removed, the directory is left as it
was, a warning is logged, and packing is not attempted again for ten
minutes.
.IP \(bu 2
The tarball, and then the directory holding it, are flushed to disk before
the packed directory is touched, so a power failure cannot leave the
directory gone and the tarball empty.
.IP \(bu 2
A packed directory is removed by first renaming it to
.IB name .removing ,
which either happens completely or not at all.
A run killed while removing it therefore never leaves a half-empty
directory under its real name, which the next run would pack again over the
complete tarball.
.IP \(bu 2
When only part of a directory can be packed, because something in it is
still being written, the instances that went into the tarball are removed
one at a time.
A run killed in the middle of that leaves some of them in place; the next
pass packs those again, into a further tarball.
The same log can then be in two tarballs, but none is lost and no tarball
is ever written over.
.IP \(bu 2
.B .partial
files and
.B .removing
directories left by a run that was killed are removed on the next start.
.IP \(bu 2
If a run was killed after the tarball was renamed into place but before the
directory was renamed away, the directory is still whole, and the next run
packs it again and replaces the tarball with an identical one.
.PP
.BR babysit (1)
never waits on any of this.
A command whose log cannot be created because the disk is full is not
started, and
.B babysit
reports the error.
.PP
The log directory can be emptied, or removed altogether, while the service
is running.
At its next pass it creates the directory, its lock file and the request
FIFO again.
Until that pass nothing listens for early requests.
.SH INSTALLATION
.RS
.nf
systemctl \-\-user daemon\-reload
systemctl \-\-user enable \-\-now babysitd
.fi
.RE
.PP
A user service starts when the user's systemd instance starts, which is at
first login unless lingering is enabled
.RB ( "loginctl enable\-linger" ).
A late start loses nothing: the logs of past days are still there,
untouched, to be archived.
.SH WITHOUT SYSTEMD
.B babysitd
does not depend on systemd; the unit file is only a convenient way to start
it.
It is an ordinary foreground program that any supervisor can run as the user
whose logs it looks after.
On FreeBSD, other BSDs and macOS there are two ways to run it from that
user's
.BR crontab (1).
.PP
As a long-running process started at boot, which keeps early requests
working:
.PP
.RS
@reboot /usr/local/bin/babysitd
.RE
.PP
Or with no long-running process at all, as a periodic one-shot:
.PP
.RS
0 */6 * * * /usr/local/bin/babysitd \-\-once
.RE
.PP
In the second form nothing listens on the request FIFO, so a
.BR babysit (1)
that has exited with status 75 is not helped until the next run; run
.B "babysitd \-\-once"
by hand to help it sooner.
Nothing is lost either way.
.SH PORTABILITY
Linux and FreeBSD are the supported systems.
macOS and the other BSDs are best effort and are not tested.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR babysit (1),
.BR babysit.conf (5)
